 ### [CGH-based parallel focal point generation](8277-cgh-based-parallel-focal-point-generation.md)
-Uses phase-only CGH to split a laser beam into multiple focal spots simultaneously, increasing voxel generation density in volumetric displays.
+Uses phase-only CGHs on an LCOS-SLM to split a laser beam into multiple focal spots simultaneously, increasing voxel generation density for volumetric displays. It differs from single-point rendering by enabling parallel voxel generation, though it requires optimization of scanning trajectories and hardware constraints.
 
 ### [Phase-Only Hologram Approximations](13999-phase-only-hologram-approximations.md)
-Methods like Amplitude Discard, Double Phase, and Binary Phase to encode complex waves into phase-only SLMs without iterative optimization.
+Methods for encoding complex-valued object waves into phase-only holograms for LCSLMs without iterative optimization. Includes Amplitude Discard (simplest), Double Phase (encodes amplitude and phase into two adjacent values), and Binary Phase (quantizes to two levels). Trades image quality and energy efficiency for computational simplicity.
+
+### [Extended Phase Range SLM Modulation](17675-extended-phase-range-slm-modulation.md)
+Mitigates depth replicas in simultaneous color holography by exploiting the wavelength-dependent phase response of LCoS SLMs. Uses SLMs with phase ranges $> 2\pi$ to break wavelength-depth ambiguity, differing from [Computational Phase Modulation](7284-computational-phase-modulation-cgh-generation.md) by addressing multi-wavelength crosstalk via physical device dispersion rather than algorithmic approximation.
+
+### SLM Phase Delay Modeling
+A model for the phase delay $\phi_\lambda$ imparted by an LCoS SLM, accounting for wavelength dispersion [S3]. The phase delay is $\phi_\lambda = \frac{2\pi d}{\lambda} n(s, \lambda)$, where $d$ is the LC layer thickness, $n(s, \lambda)$ is the refractive index controlled by digital input $s$ and dependent on $\lambda$ [S3]. Quantization errors increase with larger phase ranges, requiring at least 6 bits of granularity per $2\pi$ range to maintain PSNR/SSIM [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The naive point-wise integration scales linearly with the number of object points $M$ and hologram pixels $P$ ($O(M \cdot P)$) [G]. If ORA is used, complexity increases by a factor of $I$ (iterations) and includes FFTs ($O(P \log P)$) [S1].
-*   **Performance:** High-quality reconstruction is achievable, but iterative methods (ORA) are computationally expensive for real-time applications [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Explicit control over object point positions and intensities; handles 3D volumes directly [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** SLM spatial frequency limits restrict sub-hologram size and thus the density of object points per unit area; overlap management is complex [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When precise 3D positioning of discrete voxels is required and computational resources allow for integration or iterative optimization.
+*   **Complexity:** The naive point-wise integration scales with $O(M \cdot N)$, where $M$ is the number of object points and $N$ is the number of hologram pixels [G]. This is extremely expensive for high-resolution holograms and large scenes [G].
+*   **Memory:** Requires storing the complex hologram plane $H(\vec{p})$, size $W \times H$ complex values [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Physically accurate modeling of diffraction from point sources; supports arbitrary 3D point distributions [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally intensive; sub-hologram overlap can cause interference artifacts if not managed; phase-only conversion leads to energy loss [S2, S1].
+*   **When to use:** When high-fidelity point-source reconstruction is required and computational resources allow for direct summation [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** For real-time applications with very large scenes or high-resolution SLMs without acceleration techniques (e.g., GPU parallelization, FFT-based approximations) [G].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from Fresnel holography principles, where light propagation is modeled via phase alterations [S2]. The specific application to volumetric displays using LCSLMs and the ORA algorithm was detailed by Hayasaki et al. in 2015 [S1]. The "Lens Phase Function" and "Point-wise Integration" terminology was formalized in the context of holographic near-eye displays by Maimone et al. in 2017 [S2].
+Originating from the principles of Fresnel holography, this method was formalized in the context of holographic near-eye displays and volumetric graphics [S2, S1]. The "lens phase function" and "point-wise integration" terminology was popularized by Maimone et al. [S2] for holographic projectors. The ORA (Optimization with Random Phase Algorithm) variant was developed by Hayasaki et al. [S1] for femtosecond laser processing and volumetric graphics. The extended phase range approach was introduced to address color crosstalk in simultaneous color holography [S3].
 
